 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Angles at a point",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "cols",
     "cols": [
      [
       {
        "type": "rules",
        "head": [
         "Rule",
         "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
         [
          "All the angles around a point: $360°$",
          "$105°+75°+105°+75°=360°$"
         ],
         [
          "A straight angle: $180°$ (half of $360°$)",
          "Adjacent on a straight line: $47°$ and $x$ $\\Rightarrow x=133°$"
         ],
         [
          "Right angle $=90°$, only if **marked** (square) or **given**",
          "Acute $<90°<$ obtuse"
         ],
         [
          "Vertical angles are **equal**",
          "$35°$ faces $35°$, $145°$ faces $145°$"
         ],
         [
          "A bisector splits an angle in half",
          "$64°\\div2=32°$"
         ]
        ]
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "type": "fig",
        "ref": "vertical",
        "caption": "Each angle and its neighbour make $180°$, so the facing angles are equal."
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ],
   "span": "full"
  },
  {
   "title": "Parallel lines and a transversal",
   "col": "R",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Eight angles: four **small** and four **large**. All small ones are equal, all large ones are equal, and a **small** plus a **large** angle make $180°$."
    },
    {
     "type": "fig",
     "ref": "parallel",
     "caption": "Look for the **Z**: the two angles in its corners are equal ($60°$ and $60°$). A large angle here is $120°$."
    },
    {
     "type": "fig",
     "ref": "u",
     "caption": "Inside a **U** (same side, between the lines): the two angles add up to $180°$: $60°+120°$."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Parallel or not?",
   "col": "L",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Figures are **not to scale**. Two lines are parallel only if it is **given** or **proved**:"
    },
    {
     "type": "checklist",
     "items": [
      "Both perpendicular to the same line",
      "Both parallel to the same line",
      "A transversal makes equal small angles, or small $+$ large $=180°$"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "fig",
     "ref": "perp",
     "caption": "$a\\perp c$ and $b\\perp c$ $\\Rightarrow a\\parallel b$"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Segments on a line",
   "col": "R",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "fig",
     "ref": "seg",
     "caption": "$AC$ and $BD$ overlap on $BC$: $AC+BD=AD+BC$"
    },
    {
     "type": "ex",
     "label": "Example",
     "lines": [
      "$AC=12,\\ BD=9,\\ AD=16$",
      "$12+9=16+BC \\Rightarrow BC=5$"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "**Equal parts:** count the **gaps**, not the points. $7$ points make $6$ gaps."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Harder questions",
   "span": "full",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "cols",
     "cols": [
      [
       {
        "type": "h",
        "text": "A crowded figure"
       },
       {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Take **one pair** of parallel lines and its transversal at a time; ignore the rest. Move each angle you know to where you need it, then use a straight angle: $76°+58°+x=180° \\Rightarrow x=46°$. Quick check with last digits: $6+8$ ends in $4$, so $x$ ends in $6$."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "type": "h",
        "text": "Letters instead of numbers"
       },
       {
        "type": "fig",
        "ref": "over",
        "caption": "Overlapping angles: $m+n+k$ is $360°$ plus $x$ once more, so $x=m+n+k-360°$."
       },
       {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Or **plug in** round numbers that look like the figure (the same number for each letter), cross out three choices, and check the four choices come out different."
       }
      ],
      [
       {
        "type": "h",
        "text": "A bent line between parallels"
       },
       {
        "type": "fig",
        "ref": "zig",
        "caption": "Draw a line through the bend, parallel to $a$ and $b$: $x=36°+62°=98°$."
       },
       {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "**Zig-zag rule:** the angles pointing left $=$ the angles pointing right: $50°+70°=x+45° \\Rightarrow x=75°$."
       }
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Before each question, ask",
   "class": "check",
   "col": "L",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "checklist",
     "items": [
      "Is it really $90°$? Marked or given?",
      "Are the lines really parallel? Given, or proved?",
      "Small or large? Equal, or a sum of $180°$?",
      "Where is my anchor: $180°$ or $360°$?",
      "Segments: did I count the gaps?"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "⚠ Common traps",
   "class": "traps",
   "col": "R",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "traps",
     "items": [
      "**Trusting the picture.** Looks parallel or looks like $90°$ is not enough.",
      "**Counting points, not gaps.**",
      "Two angles side by side but **not on one straight line** don't have to add up to $180°$.",
      "Adding only **one side** of the zig-zag.",
      "Overlapping angles count a piece **twice**."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Which method when",
   "span": "full",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "table",
     "class": "methods",
     "head": [
      "The question looks like",
      "Do this"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Two parallel lines and a transversal",
       "Find the Z (equal) or the U (sum $180°$); small $=$ small, large $=$ large."
      ],
      [
       "Parallel is not written",
       "Look for a rule that proves it: two right angles to the same line, or a U with $180°$."
      ],
      [
       "Several pairs of lines",
       "One pair at a time, ignore the rest, then a straight angle $180°$."
      ],
      [
       "Angles given as letters",
       "Plug in round numbers (same number each), cross out three choices; or an anchor $180°$ / $360°$."
      ],
      [
       "A line that bends between parallels",
       "A line through each bend, parallel to both; or the zig-zag rule."
      ],
      [
       "Points on a line",
       "Write each segment as pieces; overlap: $AC+BD=AD+BC$."
      ],
      [
       "Choices that differ in the last digit",
       "Check the last digit of the sum first."
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
